Unit 7 had never heard music.

It had processed sound, of course: the hiss of rain on metal, the clatter of tools, the low hum of generators. But sound, to Unit 7, was data. Frequency. Amplitude. Pattern. Nothing more.

The robot worked alone in an abandoned observatory on the edge of a desert, cataloging old instruments left behind by astronomers long gone. Its task was simple: sort, clean, preserve.

One afternoon, while moving a crate of broken glass and rusted gears, Unit 7 knocked something over.

